                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Lootfest Wars is a small real time strategy game that I find pretty easy to get into because it does not try to bury me under a huge amount of systems. The whole idea is to take control of flags while building up an army from a hand of random cards. Money comes in automatically as the match goes on, so I can spend it on soldiers and buildings and then send them into the fight. The matches are designed to last around five minutes, which makes this feel much more like a quick strategy game than something I need to sit down with for hours.\n\nWhat I like most is how simple the controls and basic rules are. I do not have to constantly worry about gathering several different resources or managing a massive base. I am mostly thinking about where I need my units, which cards are useful right now and how I can take or defend the flags. That makes the game feel relaxed compared with a lot of RTS games. At the same time, the limited time means I still have to make decisions fairly quickly because sitting around and waiting is not going to win the match for me.\n\nThere are 22 different soldier and building types, which gives me enough options to experiment with without making the game feel overloaded. I like the random card system because it means I cannot always rely on exactly the same army setup. I have to work with what I am given and figure out how to make it useful. There are also 10 maps, and the different layouts can change how I approach the flags and where I want to send my units. The practice mode against the computer is useful too because it gives me somewhere to learn the basics without needing an online match.\n\nThe level editor is probably the feature that gives Lootfest Wars more value for me beyond its short matches. I can create my own levels and even make voxel models, then play the custom maps against the computer. That is a nice addition for such a small strategy game because it gives me something to experiment with after I have seen the standard maps. I also like that the game is completely free to play. There is not a big barrier stopping me from trying it, and the small download and simple requirements make it an easy game to keep around.\n\nThe biggest problem is that the simplicity also limits how deep the strategy can become. I enjoy the quick matches, but five minutes is not long enough for the game to develop into the kind of complicated RTS experience I can get from a much larger strategy game. The random cards can also sometimes make a match feel more dependent on what I happen to receive rather than purely on planning. I think that is acceptable because randomness is part of the design, but it does mean I am not getting an extremely precise competitive strategy game.\n\nThe online side is another limitation I have to keep in mind. The game was designed around two player matches, but it is an older and very small free game, so I cannot really treat online matchmaking as the main reason to play it. For me, the practice mode and level editor are much more useful because they give me something I can actually use whenever I want. The basic voxel presentation is also quite plain, although I think that works with the game's simple approach. I would rather have a clear battlefield than a complicated visual style that makes it harder to see what is happening.\n\nOverall, Lootfest Wars is a charming little RTS that knows exactly what it wants to be. I like the five minute matches, straightforward resource system, random cards, different unit types, practice mode and level editor. It does not have enough strategic depth to compete with the bigger RTS games I really enjoy, and the small online side limits its long term appeal, but I think the simplicity is also what makes it enjoyable. For me, this works best as a quick strategy game that I can play without having to commit to a huge campaign or spend ages learning complicated systems.\n\nRating: 6.8/10\n",
